    "type": "question",
    "label": "13",
    "prompt": "Find all perfect cubes that are one more than a prime.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$8$.\n\nFactor $n^3-1$; primality forces $n-1=1$, so $n=2$.",
      "worked": "Let $n^3=p+1$, where $p$ is prime. Then $n\\geq2$ and\n$$\\begin{align*}p&=n^3-1\\\\p&=(n-1)(n^2+n+1).\\end{align*}$$\nSince $n^2+n+1>1$, primality requires $n-1=1$. Thus $n=2$, giving $p=7$ and the perfect cube $n^3=8$. Therefore, $8$ is the only such cube.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 44
  },
